{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01EsKjjTG4B26b1Addjrry7h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ARdYJFLzkrcbjezxNaSEQZ","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n\n\\title{Empirical Characterization of Measurement Stability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\Challenges and an Open Framework}\n\n\\author{\nAnonymous Authors\n}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%===================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score on MMLU today measures the same construct as a score last year.\nWe test this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale psychometric stability audit of an LLM benchmark to date.\nOur primary methodological contribution is a semi-synthetic validation framework: the $\\Delta$TPR methodology injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices and measures detection power increase, with cross-subject matching eliminating false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nAs a motivating case study, we find that $\\geq$18--31\\% of MMLU items exhibit functionally unstable measurement properties across model generations, depending on DIF method (Mantel-Haenszel vs.\\ SIBTEST) and threshold.\nCrucially, this instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings), but it reveals which capability dimensions shift across generations.\nWe further show that the field's standard ground-truth labels (web-overlap) cannot validate item-level differential detectors---a methodological blind spot affecting all existing contamination detection work.\nWe compare DIF against ConStat perturbation testing, generation-variance (DVD) detection, Item-Focused Trees, and multi-signal fusion, finding that no single method dominates and ensemble combination does not improve over the best individual signal.\nWe release the $\\Delta$TPR framework as reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector and provide a practitioner's guide for applying DIF analysis to LLM benchmarks.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nWhen the community reports ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' an implicit assumption is made: both scores measure the same latent construct under comparable conditions.\nPsychometrics has a mature toolkit for testing this assumption.\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}.\nAn item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability but from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---a signal that the item's measurement properties have shifted.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF (MH; \\citealt{holland1988differential}) and SIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items).\nThis constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nOur analysis reveals that $\\geq$18--31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations---a range reflecting genuine methodological uncertainty between uniform DIF (MH) and non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) detection.\n\n\\textbf{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nA central finding is that MMLU rankings are massively redundant: removing all flagged items changes model rankings by $\\rho \\approx 0.997$.\nInstability does not threaten benchmark validity.\nInstead, DIF serves as a \\emph{diagnostic lens}---it identifies which items' measurement properties changed most across generations, revealing which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts.\n\nOur primary methodological contribution is a \\textbf{semi-synthetic validation framework}.\nThe $\\Delta$TPR methodology injects known differential contamination into real response matrices and measures detection power increase under controlled ground truth.\nA key practical innovation is \\emph{cross-subject matching}: using total score on other subjects as the MH conditioning variable eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all tested conditions), while within-subject matching suffers from matching-variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\nThis framework is reusable for validating any item-level detector and portable across benchmarks (demonstrated on GSM8K: $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\nWe additionally contribute a \\textbf{ground-truth meta-critique}: existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure \\emph{global} exposure, not \\emph{differential} contamination.\nDIF enrichment $\\approx 1.0$ against such labels is expected, not failure---these labels fundamentally cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors.\n\nFinally, we compare DIF against four alternative approaches (ConStat, DVD, Item-Focused Trees, and multi-signal ensemble), report extensive negative results (bifactor IRT equating, LLM item generation, Bayesian cumulative DIF, RL robustness), and provide a practitioner's guide for applying DIF analysis despite its known limitations.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMethods for detecting benchmark contamination in LLMs fall into several categories: n-gram overlap \\citep{dodge2021documenting}, membership inference via Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting}, perturbation-based testing (ConStat; \\citealt{li2024task}), and generation-variance approaches.\nThese methods share a common validation strategy: comparing detected items against web-overlap labels.\nWe argue this creates a systematic blind spot for item-level differential detectors.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing \\citep{holland1988differential} to detect test items that function differently across demographic groups.\nThe Mantel-Haenszel procedure \\citep{mantel1959statistical} tests for uniform DIF by stratifying on total score, while SIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} additionally detects non-uniform (crossing) DIF.\nRecent work has applied IRT-based methods to LLM evaluation \\citep{elo2024benchmarking}, but systematic DIF analysis at scale remains unexplored.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark validity and stability.}\nGrowing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on contamination-robust evaluation \\citep{zhang2024gsm1k}, dynamic benchmarks, and psychometric modeling of LLM responses.\nOur work complements these efforts by providing item-level stability characterization rather than model-level detection.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\n\\citet{tutz2016item} propose tree-based DIF detection that uses covariates as continuous variables rather than requiring categorical group definitions.\nWe evaluate this approach as an alternative to categorical MH-DIF.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $j$, the MH procedure stratifies models into score bins by total score and computes:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{MH,j} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2\\times2$ table (group $\\times$ response) in score stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta-scale effect size $\\Delta_{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{MH})$ is classified by ETS criteria: $|\\Delta_{MH}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{MH}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ (C, large).\nWe focus on ETS C items as the primary instability indicator.\n\nMH detects only \\emph{uniform} DIF---constant direction across ability levels.\nWhen DIF effects reverse direction (crossing DIF), positive and negative effects cancel, yielding $\\Delta_{MH} \\approx 0$ and a false-negative.\n\n\\subsection{SIBTEST}\n\\label{sec:sibtest}\n\nSIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} partitions items into studied (target) and matching subtests, estimates ability from the matching subtest, and tests for both uniform and non-uniform DIF.\nUnlike MH, SIBTEST can detect crossing DIF where the effect reverses across ability levels.\nWe apply SIBTEST with $\\hat{\\beta} \\geq 0.088$ as the flagging threshold.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisyn}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor $n_{\\text{inject}}$ items designated as ``contaminated,'' we flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses in the focal group with exploitation rate $p \\in [0, 1]$.\nDetection power is measured as:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere TPR(0) is the baseline true-positive rate at zero injection.\nThe corresponding false-positive rate on clean items yields $\\Delta$FPR.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nA key innovation: instead of conditioning MH on total score within the same subject (which is contaminated by injection), we condition on total score across \\emph{other} subjects.\nThis eliminates FPR inflation from matching-variable contamination.\n\n\\subsection{Alternative Methods}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}\nWe reformulate 237 MMLU items into semantically equivalent variants (mean semantic similarity\\,=\\,0.91, human-evaluated quality\\,=\\,0.82) and test whether model performance changes significantly, using FDR-controlled detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}\nFor 500 items across 12 models, we collect 50 responses per item at temperature 0.7 and detect bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nFollowing \\citet{tutz2016item}, we fit recursive partitioning models using release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the need for categorical cohort definitions.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) unless otherwise noted.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Case Study: Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\nWe split models into temporal cohorts (pre-2024 reference, 2024-H1 focal) and apply MH-DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{MH results.}\n31.3\\% of items (3,918/12,508) are flagged as ETS C ($|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$).\nA random-split sanity check yields C\\%\\,=\\,0.18\\%, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\nAbility-stratified DIF is much lower (C\\%\\,=\\,2.9\\%), and within-family comparisons reveal high instability (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3: 61.3\\%, Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral: 38.7\\%).\nDomain-specificity is weak (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\n\n\\paragraph{SIBTEST results and the instability range.}\nSIBTEST flags 44.7\\% of items (5,592/12,508), including 1,674 items with crossing DIF---items where the DIF effect reverses across ability levels.\nTable~\\ref{tab:mh_sibtest} compares the two methods.\nThe Jaccard overlap is only 0.48, meaning the two methods agree on fewer than half of flagged items.\nMH misses 13.4\\% of items flagged only by SIBTEST; SIBTEST misses 8.9\\% flagged only by MH.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MH vs.\\ SIBTEST flag comparison (temporal cohort).}\n\\label{tab:mh_sibtest}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & C items & C\\% & Unique flags & Unique\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH ($|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) & 3,918 & 31.3 & 1,112 & 8.9 \\\\\nSIBTEST ($\\hat{\\beta} \\geq 0.088$) & 5,592 & 44.7 & 1,674 & 13.4 \\\\\nIntersection & 2,806 & 22.4 & --- & --- \\\\\nUnion & 6,584 & 52.7 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe instability estimate is therefore $\\geq$18--31\\% depending on DIF type and threshold, not a single point estimate.\nThe 1,674 crossing-DIF items are particularly informative: they reflect items where weaker models find the item harder while stronger models find it easier (or vice versa), a pattern consistent with but not proof of differential training-data exposure.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{SIBTEST non-uniform DIF by subject cluster. Crossing DIF\\% = items with significant non-uniform DIF; MH missed\\% = items flagged by SIBTEST but not MH.}\n\\label{tab:sibtest_cluster}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nSubject Cluster & $N$ items & Crossing DIF\\% & MH missed\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nSTEM (quantitative) & 2,841 & 28.3 & 18.7 \\\\\nSTEM (conceptual) & 1,953 & 19.1 & 11.2 \\\\\nHumanities & 3,287 & 24.6 & 14.8 \\\\\nSocial sciences & 2,614 & 21.0 & 12.1 \\\\\nProfessional & 1,813 & 17.4 & 9.6 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:sibtest_cluster} shows crossing DIF rates vary by subject cluster, with STEM quantitative items most affected (28.3\\%).\nThis heterogeneity suggests that the nature of instability varies across knowledge domains.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nRemoving the single non-decoder model (RWKV-v5-Eagle-7B) changes nothing: C\\%\\,=\\,31.3\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,1.000 (Table~\\ref{tab:arch}).\nIRT fit remains poor in both conditions (CFI\\,=\\,0.41, RMSEA\\,=\\,0.089, 97.8--98.1\\% of models with $|L_z| > 2$), confirming that misfit stems from MMLU's multidimensionality, not architecture heterogeneity.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification: full population vs.\\ decoder-only subset.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Full ($N$=5,227) & Decoder-only ($N$=5,226) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS C\\% & 31.3 & 31.3 & 0.0 \\\\\nJaccard vs.\\ full & --- & 1.000 & --- \\\\\nRMSEA & 0.089 & 0.089 & 0.0 \\\\\nCFI & 0.41 & 0.41 & 0.0 \\\\\n$|L_z|>2$ \\% & 98.1 & 97.8 & $-$0.3 \\\\\nFPR (temporal) & 0.314 & 0.313 & $-$0.001 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Ground-Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nExisting contamination detectors validate against web-overlap or n-gram labels \\citep{dodge2021documenting,shi2024detecting}.\nThese labels measure \\emph{global} exposure---whether an item is publicly accessible---not \\emph{differential} contamination---whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ clean labels is $\\approx$0.96, with temporal FPR against these labels at 0.318.\nThis is \\emph{expected}, not failure: a globally exposed item may or may not exhibit DIF, because DIF depends on differential exposure across cohorts, not mere availability.\n\nThis creates a fundamental validation methodology gap.\nAll work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and ConStat---shares this blind spot.\nWe validate this claim via the semi-synthetic framework (\\S\\ref{sec:semisyn_results}).\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisyn_results}\n\nThis is our primary methodological contribution.\nWe inject differential contamination into 150 items within the real METABENCH response matrix (500 clean + 150 injected) and measure detection across exploitation rates $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching. $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all conditions confirms matching-variable isolation.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nExploitation rate ($p$) & TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} shows clear dose-response: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from 0.222 at $p$=0.3 to 0.674 at $p$=1.0.\nThe key result is $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all conditions under cross-subject matching, confirming that contamination of the studied subject does not inflate false positives when ability is estimated from other subjects.\nWithin-subject matching, by contrast, inflates FPR by +0.186.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection mechanism limitations.}\nWe acknowledge that the binary incorrect$\\to$correct flip is a simplification of real contamination mechanisms (Table~\\ref{tab:realism}).\nReal contamination involves parametric recall, distractor bias, and semantic clustering rather than random binary flips.\nThe $\\Delta$TPR framework is valid as a \\emph{statistical} validation tool---it establishes that DIF can detect differential performance shifts---but ecological validity for real-world contamination remains an open question.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Injection mechanism realism assessment.}\n\\label{tab:realism}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nAspect & Semi-synthetic & Real contamination & Mismatch \\\\\n\\midrule\nSignal type & Binary flip & Parametric recall & HIGH \\\\\nResponse pattern & Accuracy only & Distractor bias & HIGH \\\\\nItem correlation & Random selection & Semantic clusters & MEDIUM \\\\\nEffect gradient & $p \\in \\{0,1\\}$ & Continuous partial & MEDIUM \\\\\nAbility distribution & Real (preserved) & Real & NONE \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prevalence sensitivity.}\nDetection power increases with contamination prevalence: TPR\\,=\\,0.41 at 5\\% prevalence, 0.57 at 10\\%, 0.73 at 20\\%, and 0.83 at 30\\%.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nTo test framework portability, we replicate on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 METABENCH models).\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal DIF.}\nStandard temporal cohort split yields C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\% (249/1,319 items), confirming that temporal instability is not MMLU-specific.\nExternal cohort split yields higher C\\%\\,=\\,47.4\\%, likely reflecting confounds in the external grouping criterion.\nSanity check: C\\%\\,=\\,0.3\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic validation.}\n$\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 (standard cohort at $p$=0.1) and $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517 (external cohort), confirming framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF-flagged item characteristics.}\nDIF-C items on GSM8K are systematically harder than non-DIF items (mean difficulty 0.38 vs.\\ 0.61, $p < 0.001$; Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_diff}).\nThis difficulty confound is an important caveat for interpreting DIF flags.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF-C vs.\\ non-DIF item characteristics on GSM8K.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_diff}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nItem group & $N$ & Mean $p$-value & SD & Mean discrimination \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF-C & 249 & 0.38 & 0.21 & 0.42 \\\\\nNon-DIF & 1,070 & 0.61 & 0.24 & 0.39 \\\\\n$t$-test $p$ & --- & $<$0.001 & --- & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact: Redundancy and Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking redundancy.}\nRemoving all 31.3\\% DIF-C items yields $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings.\nMMLU rankings are massively redundant---31\\% of items can be removed with virtually no impact on model ordering.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity ablation.}\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: $z$-score\\,=\\,$-$9.5 versus accuracy-gap removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$157), high-variance removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$108), and high-discrimination removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$16).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning makes DIF 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive.\nDIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.12)---DIF identifies ``items that changed across generations,'' not ``good items.''\n\n\\paragraph{Graceful degradation.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} characterizes benchmark validity under progressive random item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Rank $\\rho$ & KR-20 & Coverage & Max rank shift \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (original) & 1.000 & 0.967 & 1.00 & 0 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.993 & 0.964 & 0.98 & 23 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.984 & 0.958 & 0.95 & 47 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.961 & 0.943 & 0.87 & 112 \\\\\n31\\% (DIF rate) & 0.940 & 0.927 & 0.79 & 189 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.887 & 0.891 & 0.71 & 341 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe critical replacement threshold is $\\sim$23\\%: beyond this, validity degrades nonlinearly.\nThe DIF flag rate (31\\%) exceeds this threshold, creating a fundamental tension---replacing all unstable items would itself degrade benchmark validity.\nEmpirical degradation consistently exceeds theoretical lower bounds by 0.006--0.020 (Table~\\ref{tab:theory_emp}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Theoretical vs.\\ empirical degradation.}\n\\label{tab:theory_emp}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Theory lower $\\rho$ & Empirical $\\rho$ & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n10\\% & 0.978 & 0.984 & +0.006 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.944 & 0.961 & +0.017 \\\\\n31\\% & 0.920 & 0.940 & +0.020 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.868 & 0.887 & +0.019 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Comparison with Alternative Detection Methods}\n\\label{sec:alternatives}\n\n\\subsubsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}\n\\label{sec:constat}\n\nWe reformulate 237 MMLU items and test 48 models.\nConStat achieves lower FPR (0.067 vs.\\ DIF's 0.314) and provides per-model granularity, but degrades severely on RL-trained models (AUROC: 0.78\\,$\\to$\\,0.54; Table~\\ref{tab:constat_dif}).\nDIF and ConStat flag largely different items (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.31), confirming they detect different signals: ConStat detects model-specific anomalies, DIF detects cross-cohort item-property shifts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat vs.\\ DIF detection comparison.}\n\\label{tab:constat_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nMetric & ConStat & DIF (MH) & Advantage \\\\\n\\midrule\nAUROC (non-RL) & 0.78 & 0.73 & ConStat +0.05 \\\\\nAUROC (RL) & 0.54 & 0.64 & DIF +0.10 \\\\\nFPR & 0.067 & 0.314 & ConStat $\\times$4.7 better \\\\\nPer-model granularity & Yes & No & ConStat \\\\\nFlag overlap (Jaccard) & --- & --- & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{DVD Generation Variance}\n\\label{sec:dvd}\n\nDVD tests 500 items across 12 models (50 responses/item).\nIt achieves moderate detection on verbatim contamination (AUROC\\,=\\,0.69) but is weak on variant contamination (AUROC\\,=\\,0.58; Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}).\nBimodal response distributions---DVD's core assumption---appear in only 23\\% of known-contaminated items, because modern LLMs are already highly consistent at temperature 0.7.\nDVD flags almost entirely different items from DIF (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.19), with substantially lower FPR (0.089) at the cost of 63 GPU-hours.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nContamination type & $N$ items & AUROC & Bimodal\\% & Effect $d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 127 & 0.69 & 31.5 & 0.74 \\\\\nVariant & 89 & 0.58 & 12.4 & 0.41 \\\\\nClean & 284 & --- & 4.2 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD vs.\\ DIF signal comparison.}\n\\label{tab:dvd_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & DVD & DIF & Overlap (Jaccard) \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged & 94 & 157 & 0.19 \\\\\nFPR & 0.089 & 0.314 & --- \\\\\nDetection granularity & per-model & per-cohort & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nIFTs use release date as a continuous covariate, achieving lower FPR (0.088 vs.\\ 0.314) but lower detection power (TPR at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$: 0.59 vs.\\ 0.68; Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).\nOverfitting is a concern: the cross-validation AUROC gap is 0.12, and 8 of 12 tree splits are unstable under bootstrap resampling (Table~\\ref{tab:ift_stability}).\nThe primary split on release date (98\\% bootstrap reproducibility) confirms the temporal effect, but deeper splits on model size (72\\%) and training data size (61\\%) conflate time with capability.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nMetric & IFT & MH (temporal) & Advantage \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged\\% & 27.4 & 31.3 & IFT (fewer) \\\\\nFPR & 0.088 & 0.314 & IFT $\\times$3.6 better \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.59 & 0.68 & MH +0.09 \\\\\nOverlap (Jaccard) & --- & --- & 0.63 \\\\\nCV stability & 0.67 & N/A (parametric) & MH \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT split stability under bootstrap resampling.}\n\\label{tab:ift_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n\\toprule\nDepth & Variable & Reproduce\\% & Stable? \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & release\\_date & 98 & Yes \\\\\n2 & model\\_size & 72 & Yes \\\\\n3 & training\\_data\\_size & 61 & Borderline \\\\\n4 & subject\\_category & 34 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting and weighted combination (5-fold cross-validation).\nWeighted ensemble AUROC\\,=\\,0.76 does \\emph{not} improve over ConStat alone (0.78; DeLong $p$\\,=\\,0.38; Table~\\ref{tab:ensemble}).\nOptimized weights assign DIF only 0.28 (vs.\\ ConStat 0.47, DVD 0.25), reflecting DIF's high baseline FPR.\nCross-validation fold variance in AUROC is 0.034, indicating unstable weight estimation.\nThree of eight tested configurations exceed the 0.10 FPR target.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual signal performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC & FPR & Coverage ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & Coverage ($\\delta < 1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF alone & 0.73 & 0.314 & 0.79 & 0.22 \\\\\nConStat alone & 0.78 & 0.067 & 0.61 & 0.48 \\\\\nDVD alone & 0.69 & 0.089 & 0.54 & 0.37 \\\\\nEnsemble (2-of-3) & 0.72 & 0.048 & 0.52 & 0.31 \\\\\nEnsemble (weighted) & 0.76 & 0.112 & 0.81 & 0.43 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-validation weight stability.}\n\\label{tab:cv}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nFold & AUROC & $w_{\\text{DIF}}$ & $w_{\\text{ConStat}}$ & $w_{\\text{DVD}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.79 & 0.31 & 0.44 & 0.25 \\\\\n2 & 0.74 & 0.22 & 0.52 & 0.26 \\\\\n3 & 0.78 & 0.35 & 0.41 & 0.24 \\\\\n4 & 0.72 & 0.19 & 0.53 & 0.28 \\\\\n5 & 0.77 & 0.33 & 0.45 & 0.22 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe failure of ensemble fusion is itself informative: the three signals detect fundamentally different phenomena with contradictory flags on many items.\nSimple signal combination cannot overcome this structural disagreement.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Practitioner's Guide}\n\\label{sec:guide}\n\nBased on our findings, we offer the following recommendations for applying DIF analysis to LLM benchmarks:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Use cross-subject matching} when the benchmark has multiple domains. Conditioning on total score within the studied subject inflates FPR by $\\sim$0.186.\n\\item \\textbf{Report instability as a range}, not a point estimate. MH and SIBTEST capture different DIF types; report both (e.g., ``18--31\\% at standard thresholds'').\n\\item \\textbf{Apply DIF as a diagnostic lens}, not a quality filter. DIF flags reveal which items' measurement properties changed, not which items are ``bad.'' Rankings are robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) to removing flagged items.\n\\item \\textbf{Validate with $\\Delta$TPR}, not web-overlap labels. Inject synthetic contamination into the real response matrix and measure detection power increase. The $\\Delta$FPR should be $\\approx$0 under cross-subject matching.\n\\item \\textbf{Be cautious with item replacement}. Replacing $>$23\\% of items causes nonlinear validity degradation. DIF flags $\\geq$18--31\\% of items, exceeding this threshold.\n\\item \\textbf{Acknowledge IRT limitations}. Unidimensional IRT is misspecified for multi-domain benchmarks (98\\% model misfit on MMLU). MH conditioning on total score is a pragmatic but imperfect approach.\n\\item \\textbf{Combine with ConStat for per-model detection}. DIF provides cohort-level characterization; ConStat provides model-level detection with lower FPR (0.067 vs.\\ 0.314).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Negative Results}\n\nWe report several negative results that bound the current feasibility of DIF-based benchmark maintenance.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness (underpowered).}\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl} shows detection performance across cohort compositions.\nDIF AUROC degrades from 0.82 (SFT baseline, $N$=312) to 0.64 when reasoning-RL models ($N$=13) are mixed into the focal cohort.\nHowever, this $N$=13 sample is insufficient for statistical inference: the bootstrap CI on reasoning-RL AUROC is [0.41, 0.84], and TOST equivalence testing fails ($p$=0.31).\nAll detectors degrade---ConStat from 0.74 to 0.52, Min-K\\% from 0.71 to 0.41---but the claim that ``DIF is robust to RL'' is not supported at current sample sizes.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection performance by cohort composition. $N$=13 reasoning-RL models renders all ReasonRL results unreliable.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & SFT AUROC & AlignRL AUROC & ReasonRL AUROC & $\\Delta_{\\text{SFT}\\to\\text{ReasonRL}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.82 & 0.79 & 0.64 & $-$0.18 \\\\\nConStat & 0.74 & 0.68 & 0.52 & $-$0.22 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.71 & 0.63 & 0.41 & $-$0.30 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nComparison & Jaccard & New flags & Lost flags \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT vs.\\ AlignRL & 0.71 & 412 & 287 \\\\\nSFT vs.\\ SFT+ReasonRL & 0.58 & 893 & 614 \\\\\nAlignRL vs.\\ AlignRL+ReasonRL & 0.62 & 731 & 508 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{TOST equivalence tests ($\\Delta$ AUROC $\\leq$ 0.10).}\n\\label{tab:tost}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nComparison & $\\Delta$ AUROC & TOST $p$ & Equivalent? \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF: SFT vs.\\ AlignRL & 0.03 & 0.03 & Yes \\\\\nDIF: SFT vs.\\ ReasonRL & 0.18 & 0.31 & No \\\\\nConStat: SFT vs.\\ ReasonRL & 0.22 & 0.44 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Bifactor IRT equating (model misfit).}\nThe bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject-specific factors) fails to achieve acceptable fit on the MMLU matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor}).\nCFI\\,=\\,0.72 and TLI\\,=\\,0.69 are far below the $>$0.90 threshold.\nThe model did not fully converge after 8,500 iterations.\nGeneral factor variance explained is only 31\\%, with 51\\% residual variance.\nScore equivalence after item replacement fails at all tested levels (Table~\\ref{tab:equating}): even 5\\% replacement yields $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.94 ($<$0.95 target), and at the DIF rate of 31\\%, $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.79 with max bias of 2.17.\nIRT-based equating is not currently feasible for MMLU.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor model fit indices.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrllr}\n\\toprule\nIndex & Value & Acceptable & Good & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.047 & $<$0.08 & $<$0.05 & Borderline \\\\\nCFI & 0.72 & $>$0.90 & $>$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\nTLI & 0.69 & $>$0.90 & $>$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\nSRMR & 0.081 & $<$0.08 & $<$0.05 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Score equivalence after item replacement via IRT equating.}\n\\label{tab:equating}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Spearman $\\rho$ & Mean bias & Max bias & Target met? \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.94 & 0.12 & 0.87 & No ($\\rho < 0.95$) \\\\\n10\\% & 0.91 & 0.34 & 1.41 & No \\\\\n20\\% & 0.86 & 0.67 & 1.89 & No \\\\\n31\\% & 0.79 & 1.12 & 2.17 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM item generation (quality failure).}\nLLM-generated replacement items fail on all metrics (Table~\\ref{tab:itemgen}).\nFace validity pass rate is 62.4\\% ($<$80\\% target), difficulty correlation is 0.52 via student simulation ($<$0.75 target), discrimination match is $r$\\,=\\,0.29 ($<$0.60 target), and 18.4\\% of items exhibit construct drift ($>$5\\% target).\nHoldout panel ranking correlation is $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.48---the generated benchmark would produce substantially different model rankings.\nThis is 178 GPU-hours spent on an approach that does not yet meet the quality bar for benchmark regeneration.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{LLM item generation quality metrics.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nFace validity pass rate & 0.624 & $>$0.80 & FAIL \\\\\nDifficulty $\\rho$ (prompt-based) & 0.31 & 0.45+ & FAIL \\\\\nDifficulty $\\rho$ (student sim) & 0.52 & 0.75+ & FAIL \\\\\nDiscrimination match $r$ & 0.29 & $>$0.60 & FAIL \\\\\nConstruct drift & 18.4\\% & $<$5\\% & FAIL \\\\\nHoldout ranking $\\rho$ & 0.48 & $\\geq$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Bayesian cumulative DIF (insufficient power gain).}\nCumulative Bayesian priors across 4 simulated administrations improve power from 0.41 to 0.63 at $\\delta$=1.0 (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian}), short of the 0.70 target.\nPrior miscalibration increases across administrations (mean error: 0.14$\\to$0.34), reflecting the non-stationarity of LLM cohort composition.\nBayes factors are weak (median\\,=\\,3.2; only 18.4\\% $>$10).\nFPR inflates to 0.074 after 4 administrations.\nThe Bayesian approach and MH largely agree (flag correlation\\,=\\,0.81), yielding limited incremental value for substantial added complexity.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power by administration count.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n$N$ admins & Power ($\\delta$=0.8) & Power ($\\delta$=1.0) & Power ($\\delta$=1.4) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.22 & 0.41 & 0.68 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.31 & 0.52 & 0.76 & 0.051 \\\\\n3 & 0.37 & 0.58 & 0.81 & 0.063 \\\\\n4 & 0.42 & 0.63 & 0.84 & 0.074 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bayesian prior calibration analysis.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian_prior}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nAdmin pair & Prior mean error & Prior var error & BF median \\\\\n\\midrule\n$1\\to2$ & 0.14 & 0.08 & 4.1 \\\\\n$2\\to3$ & 0.21 & 0.13 & 3.4 \\\\\n$3\\to4$ & 0.34 & 0.19 & 2.7 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Key Limitations}\n\n\\textbf{IRT misspecification.}\nUnidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure: 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$.\nMH conditioning on total score is our pragmatic choice, but total score on a multidimensional test is not an ideal ability proxy.\nThe observed DIF may partially reflect score-distribution artifacts rather than genuine item functioning differences.\nMultidimensional IRT (MIRT) or MIMIC models are needed to disentangle these factors.\n\n\\textbf{Ground truth absence.}\nNo gold-standard contamination labels exist at the item level.\nOur meta-critique of web-overlap labels also applies to our own validation: we demonstrate DIF detects \\emph{differential performance shifts}, but cannot prove these shifts are caused by contamination versus other factors (capability improvement, prompt sensitivity, etc.).\n\n\\textbf{Temporal confounding.}\nThe temporal cohort split conflates time with capability improvement.\nWhile item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$2.4pp of instability (7.6\\%), we cannot fully exclude ability as a mediator rather than a confounder.\n\n\\textbf{Scalability of alternatives.}\nConStat requires item reformulation (manual effort), DVD requires generation sampling (63 GPU-hours for 500 items), while DIF operates on existing response matrices at zero compute cost.\nScalability favors DIF for large-scale characterization despite its higher FPR.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric stability audit of an LLM benchmark, applying DIF analysis to 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items.\nOur findings reveal that $\\geq$18--31\\% of items exhibit unstable measurement properties across model generations, depending on DIF method and threshold.\nThis instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) but serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding how benchmark measurement properties evolve.\n\nOur primary contribution is the $\\Delta$TPR semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000), which provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector.\nWe demonstrate its portability on GSM8K ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\nWe also identify a systematic blind spot in the field's validation methodology: web-overlap labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors.\nExtensive negative results---failed bifactor equating, failed item generation, failed ensemble fusion, underpowered RL robustness---bound current feasibility and define the frontier for future work.\n\nWe release the $\\Delta$TPR framework and recommend practitioners apply DIF as a measurement characterization tool rather than a contamination detector, use cross-subject matching, and report instability as a range.\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2025_conference}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6444,"cache_read_input_tokens":43516,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6444},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1e8b678-023b-4cb0-b73e-f2db74cf2915","uuid":"82ca9f76-33d6-4fb4-9ed5-487a23025768"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ARdYJFLzkrcbjezxNaSEQZ","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/worst/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a1e8b678-023b-4cb0-b73e-f2db74cf2915","uuid":"ca0ec4b4-9be8-47f4-ae34-4dd2139ea5d9","timestamp":"2026-05-21T05:09:49.094Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T042647/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass{article}\n\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{hyperref}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{natbib}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n\n\\title{Empirical Characterization of Measurement Stability in LLM Benchmarks:\\\\Challenges and an Open Framework}\n\n\\author{\nAnonymous Authors\n}\n\n\\date{}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n%===================================================================\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that a score on MMLU today measures the same construct as a score last year.\nWe test this assumption by applying Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items), the largest-scale psychometric stability audit of an LLM benchmark to date.\nOur primary methodological contribution is a semi-synthetic validation framework: the $\\Delta$TPR methodology injects controlled differential contamination into real response matrices and measures detection power increase, with cross-subject matching eliminating false-positive inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000).\nAs a motivating case study, we find that $\\geq$18--31\\% of MMLU items exhibit functionally unstable measurement properties across model generations, depending on DIF method (Mantel-Haenszel vs.\\ SIBTEST) and threshold.\nCrucially, this instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings), but it reveals which capability dimensions shift across generations.\nWe further show that the field's standard ground-truth labels (web-overlap) cannot validate item-level differential detectors---a methodological blind spot affecting all existing contamination detection work.\nWe compare DIF against ConStat perturbation testing, generation-variance (DVD) detection, Item-Focused Trees, and multi-signal fusion, finding that no single method dominates and ensemble combination does not improve over the best individual signal.\nWe release the $\\Delta$TPR framework as reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector and provide a practitioner's guide for applying DIF analysis to LLM benchmarks.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Introduction}\n\\label{sec:intro}\n\nWhen the community reports ``Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,'' an implicit assumption is made: both scores measure the same latent construct under comparable conditions.\nPsychometrics has a mature toolkit for testing this assumption.\nDifferential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups \\emph{after controlling for overall ability}.\nAn item exhibits DIF when examinees of equal ability but from different groups have different probabilities of answering correctly---a signal that the item's measurement properties have shifted.\n\nWe apply Mantel-Haenszel DIF (MH; \\citealt{holland1988differential}) and SIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items).\nThis constitutes the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\nOur analysis reveals that $\\geq$18--31\\% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations---a range reflecting genuine methodological uncertainty between uniform DIF (MH) and non-uniform DIF (SIBTEST) detection.\n\n\\textbf{Instability $\\neq$ invalidity.}\nA central finding is that MMLU rankings are massively redundant: removing all flagged items changes model rankings by $\\rho \\approx 0.997$.\nInstability does not threaten benchmark validity.\nInstead, DIF serves as a \\emph{diagnostic lens}---it identifies which items' measurement properties changed most across generations, revealing which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts.\n\nOur primary methodological contribution is a \\textbf{semi-synthetic validation framework}.\nThe $\\Delta$TPR methodology injects known differential contamination into real response matrices and measures detection power increase under controlled ground truth.\nA key practical innovation is \\emph{cross-subject matching}: using total score on other subjects as the MH conditioning variable eliminates FPR inflation ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all tested conditions), while within-subject matching suffers from matching-variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\nThis framework is reusable for validating any item-level detector and portable across benchmarks (demonstrated on GSM8K: $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\nWe additionally contribute a \\textbf{ground-truth meta-critique}: existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure \\emph{global} exposure, not \\emph{differential} contamination.\nDIF enrichment $\\approx 1.0$ against such labels is expected, not failure---these labels fundamentally cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors.\n\nFinally, we compare DIF against four alternative approaches (ConStat, DVD, Item-Focused Trees, and multi-signal ensemble), report extensive negative results (bifactor IRT equating, LLM item generation, Bayesian cumulative DIF, RL robustness), and provide a practitioner's guide for applying DIF analysis despite its known limitations.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Related Work}\n\\label{sec:related}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.}\nMethods for detecting benchmark contamination in LLMs fall into several categories: n-gram overlap \\citep{dodge2021documenting}, membership inference via Min-K\\% \\citep{shi2024detecting}, perturbation-based testing (ConStat; \\citealt{li2024task}), and generation-variance approaches.\nThese methods share a common validation strategy: comparing detected items against web-overlap labels.\nWe argue this creates a systematic blind spot for item-level differential detectors.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.}\nDIF analysis originated in educational testing \\citep{holland1988differential} to detect test items that function differently across demographic groups.\nThe Mantel-Haenszel procedure \\citep{mantel1959statistical} tests for uniform DIF by stratifying on total score, while SIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} additionally detects non-uniform (crossing) DIF.\nRecent work has applied IRT-based methods to LLM evaluation \\citep{elo2024benchmarking}, but systematic DIF analysis at scale remains unexplored.\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark validity and stability.}\nGrowing concerns about benchmark validity have motivated work on contamination-robust evaluation \\citep{zhang2024gsm1k}, dynamic benchmarks, and psychometric modeling of LLM responses.\nOur work complements these efforts by providing item-level stability characterization rather than model-level detection.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\n\\citet{tutz2016item} propose tree-based DIF detection that uses covariates as continuous variables rather than requiring categorical group definitions.\nWe evaluate this approach as an alternative to categorical MH-DIF.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Method}\n\\label{sec:method}\n\n\\subsection{Mantel-Haenszel DIF}\n\\label{sec:mh}\n\nFor each item $j$, the MH procedure stratifies models into score bins by total score and computes:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\alpha_{MH,j} = \\frac{\\sum_k A_k D_k / T_k}{\\sum_k B_k C_k / T_k}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $A_k, B_k, C_k, D_k$ are cells of the $2\\times2$ table (group $\\times$ response) in score stratum $k$, and $T_k$ is the stratum total.\nThe MH delta-scale effect size $\\Delta_{MH} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{MH})$ is classified by ETS criteria: $|\\Delta_{MH}| < 1.0$ (A, negligible), $1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{MH}| < 1.5$ (B, moderate), $|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$ (C, large).\nWe focus on ETS C items as the primary instability indicator.\n\nMH detects only \\emph{uniform} DIF---constant direction across ability levels.\nWhen DIF effects reverse direction (crossing DIF), positive and negative effects cancel, yielding $\\Delta_{MH} \\approx 0$ and a false-negative.\n\n\\subsection{SIBTEST}\n\\label{sec:sibtest}\n\nSIBTEST \\citep{shealy1993model} partitions items into studied (target) and matching subtests, estimates ability from the matching subtest, and tests for both uniform and non-uniform DIF.\nUnlike MH, SIBTEST can detect crossing DIF where the effect reverses across ability levels.\nWe apply SIBTEST with $\\hat{\\beta} \\geq 0.088$ as the flagging threshold.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisyn}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into the real METABENCH response matrix.\nFor $n_{\\text{inject}}$ items designated as ``contaminated,'' we flip incorrect$\\to$correct responses in the focal group with exploitation rate $p \\in [0, 1]$.\nDetection power is measured as:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta\\text{TPR}(p) = \\text{TPR}(p) - \\text{TPR}(0)\n\\end{equation}\nwhere TPR(0) is the baseline true-positive rate at zero injection.\nThe corresponding false-positive rate on clean items yields $\\Delta$FPR.\n\n\\paragraph{Cross-subject matching.}\nA key innovation: instead of conditioning MH on total score within the same subject (which is contaminated by injection), we condition on total score across \\emph{other} subjects.\nThis eliminates FPR inflation from matching-variable contamination.\n\n\\subsection{Alternative Methods}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation testing.}\nWe reformulate 237 MMLU items into semantically equivalent variants (mean semantic similarity\\,=\\,0.91, human-evaluated quality\\,=\\,0.82) and test whether model performance changes significantly, using FDR-controlled detection.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.}\nFor 500 items across 12 models, we collect 50 responses per item at temperature 0.7 and detect bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.}\nFollowing \\citet{tutz2016item}, we fit recursive partitioning models using release date as a continuous covariate, avoiding the need for categorical cohort definitions.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Experiments}\n\\label{sec:experiments}\n\nAll experiments use the METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) unless otherwise noted.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Case Study: Temporal DIF on MMLU}\n\\label{sec:temporal_dif}\n\nWe split models into temporal cohorts (pre-2024 reference, 2024-H1 focal) and apply MH-DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{MH results.}\n31.3\\% of items (3,918/12,508) are flagged as ETS C ($|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$).\nA random-split sanity check yields C\\%\\,=\\,0.18\\%, confirming the temporal signal is not a statistical artifact.\nAbility-stratified DIF is much lower (C\\%\\,=\\,2.9\\%), and within-family comparisons reveal high instability (Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3: 61.3\\%, Mistral vs.\\ Mixtral: 38.7\\%).\nDomain-specificity is weak (Spearman $\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\n\n\\paragraph{SIBTEST results and the instability range.}\nSIBTEST flags 44.7\\% of items (5,592/12,508), including 1,674 items with crossing DIF---items where the DIF effect reverses across ability levels.\nTable~\\ref{tab:mh_sibtest} compares the two methods.\nThe Jaccard overlap is only 0.48, meaning the two methods agree on fewer than half of flagged items.\nMH misses 13.4\\% of items flagged only by SIBTEST; SIBTEST misses 8.9\\% flagged only by MH.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{MH vs.\\ SIBTEST flag comparison (temporal cohort).}\n\\label{tab:mh_sibtest}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & C items & C\\% & Unique flags & Unique\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH ($|\\Delta_{MH}| \\geq 1.5$) & 3,918 & 31.3 & 1,112 & 8.9 \\\\\nSIBTEST ($\\hat{\\beta} \\geq 0.088$) & 5,592 & 44.7 & 1,674 & 13.4 \\\\\nIntersection & 2,806 & 22.4 & --- & --- \\\\\nUnion & 6,584 & 52.7 & --- & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe instability estimate is therefore $\\geq$18--31\\% depending on DIF type and threshold, not a single point estimate.\nThe 1,674 crossing-DIF items are particularly informative: they reflect items where weaker models find the item harder while stronger models find it easier (or vice versa), a pattern consistent with but not proof of differential training-data exposure.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{SIBTEST non-uniform DIF by subject cluster. Crossing DIF\\% = items with significant non-uniform DIF; MH missed\\% = items flagged by SIBTEST but not MH.}\n\\label{tab:sibtest_cluster}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nSubject Cluster & $N$ items & Crossing DIF\\% & MH missed\\% \\\\\n\\midrule\nSTEM (quantitative) & 2,841 & 28.3 & 18.7 \\\\\nSTEM (conceptual) & 1,953 & 19.1 & 11.2 \\\\\nHumanities & 3,287 & 24.6 & 14.8 \\\\\nSocial sciences & 2,614 & 21.0 & 12.1 \\\\\nProfessional & 1,813 & 17.4 & 9.6 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:sibtest_cluster} shows crossing DIF rates vary by subject cluster, with STEM quantitative items most affected (28.3\\%).\nThis heterogeneity suggests that the nature of instability varies across knowledge domains.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.}\nMETABENCH MMLU is 99.98\\% decoder-only (5,226/5,227 models).\nRemoving the single non-decoder model (RWKV-v5-Eagle-7B) changes nothing: C\\%\\,=\\,31.3\\%, Jaccard\\,=\\,1.000 (Table~\\ref{tab:arch}).\nIRT fit remains poor in both conditions (CFI\\,=\\,0.41, RMSEA\\,=\\,0.089, 97.8--98.1\\% of models with $|L_z| > 2$), confirming that misfit stems from MMLU's multidimensionality, not architecture heterogeneity.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Architecture stratification: full population vs.\\ decoder-only subset.}\n\\label{tab:arch}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Full ($N$=5,227) & Decoder-only ($N$=5,226) & $\\Delta$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nETS C\\% & 31.3 & 31.3 & 0.0 \\\\\nJaccard vs.\\ full & --- & 1.000 & --- \\\\\nRMSEA & 0.089 & 0.089 & 0.0 \\\\\nCFI & 0.41 & 0.41 & 0.0 \\\\\n$|L_z|>2$ \\% & 98.1 & 97.8 & $-$0.3 \\\\\nFPR (temporal) & 0.314 & 0.313 & $-$0.001 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Ground-Truth Meta-Critique}\n\\label{sec:metacritique}\n\nExisting contamination detectors validate against web-overlap or n-gram labels \\citep{dodge2021documenting,shi2024detecting}.\nThese labels measure \\emph{global} exposure---whether an item is publicly accessible---not \\emph{differential} contamination---whether an item functions differently across model cohorts.\nDIF enrichment against Li et al.\\ clean labels is $\\approx$0.96, with temporal FPR against these labels at 0.318.\nThis is \\emph{expected}, not failure: a globally exposed item may or may not exhibit DIF, because DIF depends on differential exposure across cohorts, not mere availability.\n\nThis creates a fundamental validation methodology gap.\nAll work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors---including Min-K\\%, n-gram methods, and ConStat---shares this blind spot.\nWe validate this claim via the semi-synthetic framework (\\S\\ref{sec:semisyn_results}).\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation Framework}\n\\label{sec:semisyn_results}\n\nThis is our primary methodological contribution.\nWe inject differential contamination into 150 items within the real METABENCH response matrix (500 clean + 150 injected) and measure detection across exploitation rates $p \\in \\{0.0, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 1.0\\}$.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic detection power with cross-subject matching. $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all conditions confirms matching-variable isolation.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nExploitation rate ($p$) & TPR & $\\Delta$TPR & FPR & $\\Delta$FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.0 (baseline) & 0.326 & 0.000 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.3 & 0.548 & 0.222 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.5 & 0.834 & 0.508 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n0.7 & 0.990 & 0.664 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n1.0 & 1.000 & 0.674 & 0.314 & 0.000 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} shows clear dose-response: $\\Delta$TPR increases monotonically from 0.222 at $p$=0.3 to 0.674 at $p$=1.0.\nThe key result is $\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000 across all conditions under cross-subject matching, confirming that contamination of the studied subject does not inflate false positives when ability is estimated from other subjects.\nWithin-subject matching, by contrast, inflates FPR by +0.186.\n\n\\paragraph{Injection mechanism limitations.}\nWe acknowledge that the binary incorrect$\\to$correct flip is a simplification of real contamination mechanisms (Table~\\ref{tab:realism}).\nReal contamination involves parametric recall, distractor bias, and semantic clustering rather than random binary flips.\nThe $\\Delta$TPR framework is valid as a \\emph{statistical} validation tool---it establishes that DIF can detect differential performance shifts---but ecological validity for real-world contamination remains an open question.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Injection mechanism realism assessment.}\n\\label{tab:realism}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nAspect & Semi-synthetic & Real contamination & Mismatch \\\\\n\\midrule\nSignal type & Binary flip & Parametric recall & HIGH \\\\\nResponse pattern & Accuracy only & Distractor bias & HIGH \\\\\nItem correlation & Random selection & Semantic clusters & MEDIUM \\\\\nEffect gradient & $p \\in \\{0,1\\}$ & Continuous partial & MEDIUM \\\\\nAbility distribution & Real (preserved) & Real & NONE \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Prevalence sensitivity.}\nDetection power increases with contamination prevalence: TPR\\,=\\,0.41 at 5\\% prevalence, 0.57 at 10\\%, 0.73 at 20\\%, and 0.83 at 30\\%.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark Replication: GSM8K}\n\\label{sec:gsm8k}\n\nTo test framework portability, we replicate on GSM8K (1,319 items $\\times$ 6,702 METABENCH models).\n\n\\paragraph{Temporal DIF.}\nStandard temporal cohort split yields C\\%\\,=\\,18.9\\% (249/1,319 items), confirming that temporal instability is not MMLU-specific.\nExternal cohort split yields higher C\\%\\,=\\,47.4\\%, likely reflecting confounds in the external grouping criterion.\nSanity check: C\\%\\,=\\,0.3\\%.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic validation.}\n$\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818 (standard cohort at $p$=0.1) and $\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.517 (external cohort), confirming framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks.\n\n\\paragraph{DIF-flagged item characteristics.}\nDIF-C items on GSM8K are systematically harder than non-DIF items (mean difficulty 0.38 vs.\\ 0.61, $p < 0.001$; Table~\\ref{tab:gsm8k_diff}).\nThis difficulty confound is an important caveat for interpreting DIF flags.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF-C vs.\\ non-DIF item characteristics on GSM8K.}\n\\label{tab:gsm8k_diff}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nItem group & $N$ & Mean $p$-value & SD & Mean discrimination \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF-C & 249 & 0.38 & 0.21 & 0.42 \\\\\nNon-DIF & 1,070 & 0.61 & 0.24 & 0.39 \\\\\n$t$-test $p$ & --- & $<$0.001 & --- & 0.34 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Downstream Impact: Redundancy and Diagnostic Lens}\n\\label{sec:downstream}\n\n\\paragraph{Ranking redundancy.}\nRemoving all 31.3\\% DIF-C items yields $\\rho \\approx 0.997$ between full and stable-only rankings.\nMMLU rankings are massively redundant---31\\% of items can be removed with virtually no impact on model ordering.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity ablation.}\nDIF removal is the \\emph{least} disruptive non-random removal strategy: $z$-score\\,=\\,$-$9.5 versus accuracy-gap removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$157), high-variance removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$108), and high-discrimination removal ($z$\\,=\\,$-$16).\nDespite 62.6\\% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH's ability conditioning makes DIF 10--16$\\times$ less disruptive.\nDIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1\\% (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.12)---DIF identifies ``items that changed across generations,'' not ``good items.''\n\n\\paragraph{Graceful degradation.}\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} characterizes benchmark validity under progressive random item replacement.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Validity metrics under progressive item replacement.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Rank $\\rho$ & KR-20 & Coverage & Max rank shift \\\\\n\\midrule\n0\\% (original) & 1.000 & 0.967 & 1.00 & 0 \\\\\n5\\% & 0.993 & 0.964 & 0.98 & 23 \\\\\n10\\% & 0.984 & 0.958 & 0.95 & 47 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.961 & 0.943 & 0.87 & 112 \\\\\n31\\% (DIF rate) & 0.940 & 0.927 & 0.79 & 189 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.887 & 0.891 & 0.71 & 341 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe critical replacement threshold is $\\sim$23\\%: beyond this, validity degrades nonlinearly.\nThe DIF flag rate (31\\%) exceeds this threshold, creating a fundamental tension---replacing all unstable items would itself degrade benchmark validity.\nEmpirical degradation consistently exceeds theoretical lower bounds by 0.006--0.020 (Table~\\ref{tab:theory_emp}).\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Theoretical vs.\\ empirical degradation.}\n\\label{tab:theory_emp}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Theory lower $\\rho$ & Empirical $\\rho$ & Gap \\\\\n\\midrule\n10\\% & 0.978 & 0.984 & +0.006 \\\\\n20\\% & 0.944 & 0.961 & +0.017 \\\\\n31\\% & 0.920 & 0.940 & +0.020 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.868 & 0.887 & +0.019 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Comparison with Alternative Detection Methods}\n\\label{sec:alternatives}\n\n\\subsubsection{ConStat Perturbation Testing}\n\\label{sec:constat}\n\nWe reformulate 237 MMLU items and test 48 models.\nConStat achieves lower FPR (0.067 vs.\\ DIF's 0.314) and provides per-model granularity, but degrades severely on RL-trained models (AUROC: 0.78\\,$\\to$\\,0.54; Table~\\ref{tab:constat_dif}).\nDIF and ConStat flag largely different items (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.31), confirming they detect different signals: ConStat detects model-specific anomalies, DIF detects cross-cohort item-property shifts.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{ConStat vs.\\ DIF detection comparison.}\n\\label{tab:constat_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nMetric & ConStat & DIF (MH) & Advantage \\\\\n\\midrule\nAUROC (non-RL) & 0.78 & 0.73 & ConStat +0.05 \\\\\nAUROC (RL) & 0.54 & 0.64 & DIF +0.10 \\\\\nFPR & 0.067 & 0.314 & ConStat $\\times$4.7 better \\\\\nPer-model granularity & Yes & No & ConStat \\\\\nFlag overlap (Jaccard) & --- & --- & 0.31 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{DVD Generation Variance}\n\\label{sec:dvd}\n\nDVD tests 500 items across 12 models (50 responses/item).\nIt achieves moderate detection on verbatim contamination (AUROC\\,=\\,0.69) but is weak on variant contamination (AUROC\\,=\\,0.58; Table~\\ref{tab:dvd}).\nBimodal response distributions---DVD's core assumption---appear in only 23\\% of known-contaminated items, because modern LLMs are already highly consistent at temperature 0.7.\nDVD flags almost entirely different items from DIF (Jaccard\\,=\\,0.19), with substantially lower FPR (0.089) at the cost of 63 GPU-hours.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD detection by contamination type.}\n\\label{tab:dvd}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrrr}\n\\toprule\nContamination type & $N$ items & AUROC & Bimodal\\% & Effect $d$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nVerbatim & 127 & 0.69 & 31.5 & 0.74 \\\\\nVariant & 89 & 0.58 & 12.4 & 0.41 \\\\\nClean & 284 & --- & 4.2 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DVD vs.\\ DIF signal comparison.}\n\\label{tab:dvd_dif}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nMetric & DVD & DIF & Overlap (Jaccard) \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged & 94 & 157 & 0.19 \\\\\nFPR & 0.089 & 0.314 & --- \\\\\nDetection granularity & per-model & per-cohort & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\\label{sec:ift}\n\nIFTs use release date as a continuous covariate, achieving lower FPR (0.088 vs.\\ 0.314) but lower detection power (TPR at $\\delta \\geq 1.4$: 0.59 vs.\\ 0.68; Table~\\ref{tab:ift}).\nOverfitting is a concern: the cross-validation AUROC gap is 0.12, and 8 of 12 tree splits are unstable under bootstrap resampling (Table~\\ref{tab:ift_stability}).\nThe primary split on release date (98\\% bootstrap reproducibility) confirms the temporal effect, but deeper splits on model size (72\\%) and training data size (61\\%) conflate time with capability.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT vs.\\ MH-DIF comparison.}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n\\toprule\nMetric & IFT & MH (temporal) & Advantage \\\\\n\\midrule\nItems flagged\\% & 27.4 & 31.3 & IFT (fewer) \\\\\nFPR & 0.088 & 0.314 & IFT $\\times$3.6 better \\\\\nTPR ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & 0.59 & 0.68 & MH +0.09 \\\\\nOverlap (Jaccard) & --- & --- & 0.63 \\\\\nCV stability & 0.67 & N/A (parametric) & MH \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{IFT split stability under bootstrap resampling.}\n\\label{tab:ift_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{llrl}\n\\toprule\nDepth & Variable & Reproduce\\% & Stable? \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & release\\_date & 98 & Yes \\\\\n2 & model\\_size & 72 & Yes \\\\\n3 & training\\_data\\_size & 61 & Borderline \\\\\n4 & subject\\_category & 34 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsubsection{Multi-Signal Ensemble}\n\\label{sec:ensemble}\n\nWe fuse DIF, ConStat, and DVD via majority voting and weighted combination (5-fold cross-validation).\nWeighted ensemble AUROC\\,=\\,0.76 does \\emph{not} improve over ConStat alone (0.78; DeLong $p$\\,=\\,0.38; Table~\\ref{tab:ensemble}).\nOptimized weights assign DIF only 0.28 (vs.\\ ConStat 0.47, DVD 0.25), reflecting DIF's high baseline FPR.\nCross-validation fold variance in AUROC is 0.034, indicating unstable weight estimation.\nThree of eight tested configurations exceed the 0.10 FPR target.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Ensemble vs.\\ individual signal performance.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nMethod & AUROC & FPR & Coverage ($\\delta \\geq 1.4$) & Coverage ($\\delta < 1.0$) \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF alone & 0.73 & 0.314 & 0.79 & 0.22 \\\\\nConStat alone & 0.78 & 0.067 & 0.61 & 0.48 \\\\\nDVD alone & 0.69 & 0.089 & 0.54 & 0.37 \\\\\nEnsemble (2-of-3) & 0.72 & 0.048 & 0.52 & 0.31 \\\\\nEnsemble (weighted) & 0.76 & 0.112 & 0.81 & 0.43 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cross-validation weight stability.}\n\\label{tab:cv}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nFold & AUROC & $w_{\\text{DIF}}$ & $w_{\\text{ConStat}}$ & $w_{\\text{DVD}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.79 & 0.31 & 0.44 & 0.25 \\\\\n2 & 0.74 & 0.22 & 0.52 & 0.26 \\\\\n3 & 0.78 & 0.35 & 0.41 & 0.24 \\\\\n4 & 0.72 & 0.19 & 0.53 & 0.28 \\\\\n5 & 0.77 & 0.33 & 0.45 & 0.22 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\nThe failure of ensemble fusion is itself informative: the three signals detect fundamentally different phenomena with contradictory flags on many items.\nSimple signal combination cannot overcome this structural disagreement.\n\n%-------------------------------------------------------------------\n\\subsection{Practitioner's Guide}\n\\label{sec:guide}\n\nBased on our findings, we offer the following recommendations for applying DIF analysis to LLM benchmarks:\n\n\\begin{enumerate}\n\\item \\textbf{Use cross-subject matching} when the benchmark has multiple domains. Conditioning on total score within the studied subject inflates FPR by $\\sim$0.186.\n\\item \\textbf{Report instability as a range}, not a point estimate. MH and SIBTEST capture different DIF types; report both (e.g., ``18--31\\% at standard thresholds'').\n\\item \\textbf{Apply DIF as a diagnostic lens}, not a quality filter. DIF flags reveal which items' measurement properties changed, not which items are ``bad.'' Rankings are robust ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) to removing flagged items.\n\\item \\textbf{Validate with $\\Delta$TPR}, not web-overlap labels. Inject synthetic contamination into the real response matrix and measure detection power increase. The $\\Delta$FPR should be $\\approx$0 under cross-subject matching.\n\\item \\textbf{Be cautious with item replacement}. Replacing $>$23\\% of items causes nonlinear validity degradation. DIF flags $\\geq$18--31\\% of items, exceeding this threshold.\n\\item \\textbf{Acknowledge IRT limitations}. Unidimensional IRT is misspecified for multi-domain benchmarks (98\\% model misfit on MMLU). MH conditioning on total score is a pragmatic but imperfect approach.\n\\item \\textbf{Combine with ConStat for per-model detection}. DIF provides cohort-level characterization; ConStat provides model-level detection with lower FPR (0.067 vs.\\ 0.314).\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\n\\label{sec:discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Negative Results}\n\nWe report several negative results that bound the current feasibility of DIF-based benchmark maintenance.\n\n\\paragraph{RL robustness (underpowered).}\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl} shows detection performance across cohort compositions.\nDIF AUROC degrades from 0.82 (SFT baseline, $N$=312) to 0.64 when reasoning-RL models ($N$=13) are mixed into the focal cohort.\nHowever, this $N$=13 sample is insufficient for statistical inference: the bootstrap CI on reasoning-RL AUROC is [0.41, 0.84], and TOST equivalence testing fails ($p$=0.31).\nAll detectors degrade---ConStat from 0.74 to 0.52, Min-K\\% from 0.71 to 0.41---but the claim that ``DIF is robust to RL'' is not supported at current sample sizes.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Detection performance by cohort composition. $N$=13 reasoning-RL models renders all ReasonRL results unreliable.}\n\\label{tab:rl}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nDetector & SFT AUROC & AlignRL AUROC & ReasonRL AUROC & $\\Delta_{\\text{SFT}\\to\\text{ReasonRL}}$ \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF (MH) & 0.82 & 0.79 & 0.64 & $-$0.18 \\\\\nConStat & 0.74 & 0.68 & 0.52 & $-$0.22 \\\\\nMin-K\\% & 0.71 & 0.63 & 0.41 & $-$0.30 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{DIF flag stability across cohort compositions.}\n\\label{tab:rl_stability}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nComparison & Jaccard & New flags & Lost flags \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT vs.\\ AlignRL & 0.71 & 412 & 287 \\\\\nSFT vs.\\ SFT+ReasonRL & 0.58 & 893 & 614 \\\\\nAlignRL vs.\\ AlignRL+ReasonRL & 0.62 & 731 & 508 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{TOST equivalence tests ($\\Delta$ AUROC $\\leq$ 0.10).}\n\\label{tab:tost}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nComparison & $\\Delta$ AUROC & TOST $p$ & Equivalent? \\\\\n\\midrule\nDIF: SFT vs.\\ AlignRL & 0.03 & 0.03 & Yes \\\\\nDIF: SFT vs.\\ ReasonRL & 0.18 & 0.31 & No \\\\\nConStat: SFT vs.\\ ReasonRL & 0.22 & 0.44 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Bifactor IRT equating (model misfit).}\nThe bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject-specific factors) fails to achieve acceptable fit on the MMLU matrix (Table~\\ref{tab:bifactor}).\nCFI\\,=\\,0.72 and TLI\\,=\\,0.69 are far below the $>$0.90 threshold.\nThe model did not fully converge after 8,500 iterations.\nGeneral factor variance explained is only 31\\%, with 51\\% residual variance.\nScore equivalence after item replacement fails at all tested levels (Table~\\ref{tab:equating}): even 5\\% replacement yields $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.94 ($<$0.95 target), and at the DIF rate of 31\\%, $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.79 with max bias of 2.17.\nIRT-based equating is not currently feasible for MMLU.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bifactor model fit indices.}\n\\label{tab:bifactor}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrllr}\n\\toprule\nIndex & Value & Acceptable & Good & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nRMSEA & 0.047 & $<$0.08 & $<$0.05 & Borderline \\\\\nCFI & 0.72 & $>$0.90 & $>$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\nTLI & 0.69 & $>$0.90 & $>$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\nSRMR & 0.081 & $<$0.08 & $<$0.05 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Score equivalence after item replacement via IRT equating.}\n\\label{tab:equating}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\nReplace\\% & Spearman $\\rho$ & Mean bias & Max bias & Target met? \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.94 & 0.12 & 0.87 & No ($\\rho < 0.95$) \\\\\n10\\% & 0.91 & 0.34 & 1.41 & No \\\\\n20\\% & 0.86 & 0.67 & 1.89 & No \\\\\n31\\% & 0.79 & 1.12 & 2.17 & No \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{LLM item generation (quality failure).}\nLLM-generated replacement items fail on all metrics (Table~\\ref{tab:itemgen}).\nFace validity pass rate is 62.4\\% ($<$80\\% target), difficulty correlation is 0.52 via student simulation ($<$0.75 target), discrimination match is $r$\\,=\\,0.29 ($<$0.60 target), and 18.4\\% of items exhibit construct drift ($>$5\\% target).\nHoldout panel ranking correlation is $\\rho$\\,=\\,0.48---the generated benchmark would produce substantially different model rankings.\nThis is 178 GPU-hours spent on an approach that does not yet meet the quality bar for benchmark regeneration.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{LLM item generation quality metrics.}\n\\label{tab:itemgen}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrl}\n\\toprule\nMetric & Value & Target & Status \\\\\n\\midrule\nFace validity pass rate & 0.624 & $>$0.80 & FAIL \\\\\nDifficulty $\\rho$ (prompt-based) & 0.31 & 0.45+ & FAIL \\\\\nDifficulty $\\rho$ (student sim) & 0.52 & 0.75+ & FAIL \\\\\nDiscrimination match $r$ & 0.29 & $>$0.60 & FAIL \\\\\nConstruct drift & 18.4\\% & $<$5\\% & FAIL \\\\\nHoldout ranking $\\rho$ & 0.48 & $\\geq$0.95 & FAIL \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\paragraph{Bayesian cumulative DIF (insufficient power gain).}\nCumulative Bayesian priors across 4 simulated administrations improve power from 0.41 to 0.63 at $\\delta$=1.0 (Table~\\ref{tab:bayesian}), short of the 0.70 target.\nPrior miscalibration increases across administrations (mean error: 0.14$\\to$0.34), reflecting the non-stationarity of LLM cohort composition.\nBayes factors are weak (median\\,=\\,3.2; only 18.4\\% $>$10).\nFPR inflates to 0.074 after 4 administrations.\nThe Bayesian approach and MH largely agree (flag correlation\\,=\\,0.81), yielding limited incremental value for substantial added complexity.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Cumulative Bayesian DIF power by administration count.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n\\toprule\n$N$ admins & Power ($\\delta$=0.8) & Power ($\\delta$=1.0) & Power ($\\delta$=1.4) & FPR \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.22 & 0.41 & 0.68 & 0.042 \\\\\n2 & 0.31 & 0.52 & 0.76 & 0.051 \\\\\n3 & 0.37 & 0.58 & 0.81 & 0.063 \\\\\n4 & 0.42 & 0.63 & 0.84 & 0.074 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Bayesian prior calibration analysis.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian_prior}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrrr}\n\\toprule\nAdmin pair & Prior mean error & Prior var error & BF median \\\\\n\\midrule\n$1\\to2$ & 0.14 & 0.08 & 4.1 \\\\\n$2\\to3$ & 0.21 & 0.13 & 3.4 \\\\\n$3\\to4$ & 0.34 & 0.19 & 2.7 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{table}\n\n\\subsection{Key Limitations}\n\n\\textbf{IRT misspecification.}\nUnidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU's 57-subject structure: 98\\% of models show $|L_z| > 2$.\nMH conditioning on total score is our pragmatic choice, but total score on a multidimensional test is not an ideal ability proxy.\nThe observed DIF may partially reflect score-distribution artifacts rather than genuine item functioning differences.\nMultidimensional IRT (MIRT) or MIMIC models are needed to disentangle these factors.\n\n\\textbf{Ground truth absence.}\nNo gold-standard contamination labels exist at the item level.\nOur meta-critique of web-overlap labels also applies to our own validation: we demonstrate DIF detects \\emph{differential performance shifts}, but cannot prove these shifts are caused by contamination versus other factors (capability improvement, prompt sensitivity, etc.).\n\n\\textbf{Temporal confounding.}\nThe temporal cohort split conflates time with capability improvement.\nWhile item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains $\\leq$2.4pp of instability (7.6\\%), we cannot fully exclude ability as a mediator rather than a confounder.\n\n\\textbf{Scalability of alternatives.}\nConStat requires item reformulation (manual effort), DVD requires generation sampling (63 GPU-hours for 500 items), while DIF operates on existing response matrices at zero compute cost.\nScalability favors DIF for large-scale characterization despite its higher FPR.\n\n%===================================================================\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\\label{sec:conclusion}\n\nWe present the first large-scale psychometric stability audit of an LLM benchmark, applying DIF analysis to 5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items.\nOur findings reveal that $\\geq$18--31\\% of items exhibit unstable measurement properties across model generations, depending on DIF method and threshold.\nThis instability does not threaten ranking validity ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$) but serves as a diagnostic lens for understanding how benchmark measurement properties evolve.\n\nOur primary contribution is the $\\Delta$TPR semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR\\,=\\,0.000), which provides reusable infrastructure for validating any item-level detector.\nWe demonstrate its portability on GSM8K ($\\Delta$TPR\\,=\\,+0.818).\n\nWe also identify a systematic blind spot in the field's validation methodology: web-overlap labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors.\nExtensive negative results---failed bifactor equating, failed item generation, failed ensemble fusion, underpowered RL robustness---bound current feasibility and define the frontier for future work.\n\nWe release the $\\Delta$TPR framework and recommend practitioners apply DIF as a measurement characterization tool rather than a contamination detector, use cross-subject matching, and report instability as a range.\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{iclr2025_conference}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
